                <a href=3D"https://hf.co/datasets/nyu-visionx/VSI-Train-10k=
" class=3D"btn btn-secondary">
                    <i class=3D"fa-solid fa-database"></i> VSI-Train-10k
                </a>
                <a href=3D"https://hf.co/datasets/nyu-visionx/VSI-Bench" cl=
ass=3D"btn btn-secondary">
                    <i class=3D"fa-solid fa-database"></i> VSI-Bench (Debia=
sed)
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </header>


    <!-- The Problem -->
    <section class=3D"section narrative-section">
        <div class=3D"container narrative-content">
            <div class=3D"narrative-text fade-in-up visible">
                <h3>The Dirty Secret</h3>
                <p>
                    Many multimodal benchmarks hide a dirty secret: <strong=
>models can achieve high scores without even
                        using the visual input.</strong>
                </p>
                <p>
                    This is particularly problematic for vision-centric ben=
chmarks, which explicitly aim to require
                    visual inputs to be solved. If a model can answer corre=
ctly by guessing "2" because 50% of the
                    answers are "2", are we really measuring visual underst=
anding?
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"narrative-visual fade-in-up visible">
                <!-- Carousel -->
                <div class=3D"carousel-container">
                    <div class=3D"carousel-slides-wrapper">
                        <div class=3D"carousel-slide active" data-caption=
=3D"Severe answer skew. Always guessing '2' works well.">
                            <img src=3D"http://[::]:8002/static/img/bias_a.=
jpeg" alt=3D"Counting Bias">
                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"carousel-slide" data-caption=3D"'Keyb=
oard' is the correct answer 100% of the time.">
                            <img src=3D"http://[::]:8002/static/img/bias_b.=
jpeg" alt=3D"Spatial Relation Bias">
                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"carousel-slide" data-caption=3D"'Cloc=
k' always appears in the 4th position.">
                            <img src=3D"http://[::]:8002/static/img/bias_c.=
jpeg" alt=3D"Appearance Order Bias">
                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"carousel-slide" data-caption=3D"Room =
sizes cluster predictably around 17m=C2=B2.">
                            <img src=3D"http://[::]:8002/static/img/bias_d.=
jpeg" alt=3D"Size Estimation Bias">
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class=3D"carousel-controls">
                        <button class=3D"carousel-btn prev-btn"><i class=3D=
"fa-solid fa-chevron-left"></i></button>
                        <div class=3D"carousel-center-content">
                            <div class=3D"carousel-caption-text">Severe ans=
wer skew. Always guessing '2' works well.</div>
                            <div class=3D"carousel-nav">
                                <span class=3D"carousel-dot active"></span>
                                <span class=3D"carousel-dot"></span>
                                <span class=3D"carousel-dot"></span>
                                <span class=3D"carousel-dot"></span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <button class=3D"carousel-btn next-btn"><i class=3D=
"fa-solid fa-chevron-right"></i></button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Principle -->
    <section class=3D"section" style=3D"background: var(--surface-color);">
        <div class=3D"container" style=3D"text-align: center;">
            <div class=3D"fade-in-up visible">
                <h2 style=3D"margin-bottom: 2rem;">Key Principle</h2>
                <blockquote style=3D"font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; col=
or: var(--text-primary); max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
                    "If a benchmark CAN be gamed, it WILL be."
                </blockquote>
                <p style=3D"margin-top: 2rem;">
                    Therefore, multimodal benchmark designers should proact=
ively try to "game" their own benchmarks
                    first as a key step in the development lifecycle.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Solution: TsT -->
    <section class=3D"section narrative-section">
        <div class=3D"container narrative-content">
            <div class=3D"narrative-text fade-in-up visible">
                <span class=3D"hero-tag">Diagnose</span>
                <h3>Introducing TsT</h3>
                <p>
                    <strong>Test-Set Stress-Test (TsT)</strong> is a diagno=
stic framework that quantifies non-visual
                    exploitability.
                </p>
                <p>
                    We use <strong>k-fold cross-validation DIRECTLY on the =
test set</strong> (using only non-visual text
                    inputs). This allows us to train diagnostic models that=
:
                </p>
                <ul style=3D"list-style: none; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                    <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"><i class=3D"fa-sol=
id fa-check" style=3D"color: var(--accent-primary); margin-right: 10px;"></=
i> Quantify global non-visual
                        solvability</li>
                    <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"><i class=3D"fa-sol=
id fa-check" style=3D"color: var(--accent-primary); margin-right: 10px;"></=
i> Compute per-sample bias
                        scores s(x)</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    This effectively "trains on the test set" not to overfi=
t, but to adversarially probe for intrinsic
                    vulnerabilities.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"narrative-visual fade-in-up visible">
                <img src=3D"http://[::]:8002/static/img/TsT_overview.png" a=
lt=3D"TsT Framework Overview" style=3D"background: white; padding: 1rem; bo=
rder-radius: 8px;">
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why the Test Set? -->
    <section class=3D"section narrative-section" style=3D"background: var(-=
-surface-color);">
        <div class=3D"container narrative-content split-50-50">
            <div class=3D"narrative-visual fade-in-up visible">
                <img src=3D"http://[::]:8002/static/img/TsT_overview_bias_s=
pace.png" alt=3D"Bias Space Diagram" style=3D"background: white; padding: 1=
rem; border-radius: 8px;">
            </div>
            <div class=3D"narrative-text fade-in-up visible">
                <!-- <h3>It's Not Just Overfitting</h3> -->
                <h3>Why the <em>test set specifically?</em></h3>
                <p>
                    <!-- You might ask: <em>"Is it actually crucial to 'tra=
in' on the test set specifically?"</em> -->
                    Is this crucial to target the <em>test set</em> itself?=
 Instead of some held-out "train"
                    set...
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>YES!</strong> Once a benchmark is released, mod=
els can exploit the <em>SPECIFIC</em>
                    artifacts present in <em>THAT</em> test set=E2=80=94idi=
osyncrasies of sampling, templates, or curation.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Training on a held-out "train" set only captures genera=
l domain biases (<span style=3D"color: #2A2AFF;">blue region</span>). To fi=
nd the
                    shortcuts that break your specific benchmark, you must =
<strong>stress-test the actual test
                        instrument itself</strong> (<span style=3D"color: #=
FF0066;">pink region</span>).
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Diagnosis Results -->
    <section class=3D"section">
        <div class=3D"container">
            <h2 style=3D"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Diagnosi=
s: Shocking Vulnerabilities</h2>
            <p style=3D"text-align: center; max-width: 700px; margin: 0 aut=
o 3rem auto; color: var(--text-secondary);">
                When we applied TsT to leading benchmarks, the results were=
 alarming. Models could solve significant
                portions of the test sets <strong>without seeing the images=
</strong>.
            </p>

            <!-- High Level Stats -->
            <div class=3D"grid fade-in-up visible" style=3D"margin-bottom: =
4rem;">
                <div class=3D"stat-card">
                    <div class=3D"stat-number">+33%</div>
                    <div class=3D"stat-label">CV-Bench Blind Gain</div>
                    <p style=3D"font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1rem;">Blind=
 accuracy jumped from 40% to 73% using TsT.</p>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"stat-card">
                    <div class=3D"stat-number">+31%</div>
                    <div class=3D"stat-label">VSI-Bench Blind Gain</div>
                    <p style=3D"font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1rem;">Blind=
 accuracy jumped from 25% to 56% using TsT.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    <!-- Mitigation -->
    <!-- <section class=3D"section narrative-section" style=3D"background: =
var(--surface-color);"> -->
    <section class=3D"section narrative-section">
        <div class=3D"container narrative-content">
            <div class=3D"narrative-text fade-in-up visible">
                <span class=3D"hero-tag">Debias</span>
                <h2>Iterative Bias Pruning (IBP)</h2>
                <p>
                    Identifying the problem isn't enough. We need to fix it=
. We use TsT's bias scores to systematically
                    debias benchmarks.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Iterative Bias Pruning (IBP)</strong> alternate=
s between:
                </p>
                <ol style=3D"margin-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--text-seconda=
ry); margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                    <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Pruning the top-k =
most biased samples</li>
                    <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Re-calculating bia=
s scores via TsT</li>
                </ol>
                <p>
                    The result? <strong>VSI-Bench-Debiased</strong>, a robu=
st subset that is much harder to game via
                    shortcuts.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"narrative-visual fade-in-up visible">
                <!-- Placeholder for IBP visual if available, or reuse a re=
levant chart -->
                <div style=3D"background: #111; padding: 2rem; border-radiu=
s: 12px; text-align: center;">
                    <i class=3D"fa-solid fa-scissors" style=3D"font-size: 4=
rem; color: var(--accent-secondary); margin-bottom: 1rem;"></i>
                    <h4>Systematic Debiasing</h4>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Mitigation Results -->
    <section class=3D"section">
        <div class=3D"container">
            <h2 style=3D"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Proof it=
 Works</h2>
            <p style=3D"text-align: center; max-width: 700px; margin: 0 aut=
o 3rem auto; color: var(--text-secondary);">
                By applying the full TsT+IBP procedure to VSI-Bench, we cre=
ated a debiased version where visual
                understanding is actually required.
            </p>

            <!-- High Level Stats -->
            <div class=3D"grid fade-in-up visible" style=3D"margin-bottom: =
4rem; justify-content: center;">
                <div class=3D"stat-card">
                    <div class=3D"stat-number">34%</div>
                    <div class=3D"stat-label">Wider Gap</div>
                    <p style=3D"font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1rem;">VSI-B=
ench-Debiased shows a much larger vision-blind
                        performance gap.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Detailed Table -->
            <div class=3D"fade-in-up visible" style=3D"max-width: 900px; ma=
rgin: 0 auto;">
                <h3 style=3D"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Ca=
se Study: VSI-Bench-Debiased</h3>
                <div class=3D"data-table-container">
                    <table class=3D"data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Model Configuration</th>
                                <th>Vision</th>
                                <th>Blind</th>
                                <th>Gap (V-B)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td colspan=3D"4" style=3D"background: rgba=
(255,255,255,0.02); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-secondary);">
                                    Original VSI-Bench</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>LLaVA-Video 7B (Base)</td>
                                <td>36.7</td>
                                <td>25.9</td>
                                <td>10.8</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>+ VSI-Train-10k FT</td>
                                <td>57.1</td>
                                <td>44.7</td>
                                <td>12.4</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td colspan=3D"4" style=3D"background: rgba=
(255,255,255,0.02); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-secondary);">
                                    VSI-Bench-Debiased (Ours)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>LLaVA-Video 7B (Base)</td>
                                <td>31.3</td>
                                <td>20.3</td>
                                <td>11.0</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>+ VSI-Train-10k FT</td>
                                <td>48.7</td>
                                <td>32.0</td>
                                <td class=3D"highlight-positive">16.6</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style=3D"text-align: center; font-size: 0.9rem; color: v=
ar(--text-secondary);">
                    <em>On the debiased benchmark, the blind model struggle=
s significantly more (44.7% =E2=86=92
                        32.0%), proving that shortcuts have been removed.</=
em>
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

